# Figma

This project's Figma tool. **One file — {{FIGMA_FILE}}** (team **{{TEAM}}**, folder **{{FOLDER}}**) — holds both the reusable components and the design work.

Figma calls a folder a **Folder**. It was called a Project until 2025, and the REST API still says `projects` in its paths and its responses. Read an API path as written; say "folder" to a person.

## Built on figma-use

This skill is an **overlay** on [figma-use](../figma-use/SKILL.md), Figma's official skill for driving the MCP server. `figma-use` owns the plugin-API mechanics — the `use_figma` tool, page loading, font loading, query/set helpers, error recovery. This skill owns only the project's conventions.

**Whenever an operation here requires programmatic execution in Figma, follow `figma-use` for how to drive the API.** This skill never duplicates that material.

## Owned operations

- **read design tokens** — query Figma Variables for colors, typography, spacing.
- **read design component** — query components and their variants.
- **add design token** — add a variable.
- **add design component** — add a reusable component.
- **publish design library** — publish the file as a library (only needed when something outside it consumes the components).
- **open design file** — open the project's file.
- **comment on design** — Figma comments or sticky annotations.
- **mark frame ready for development** — apply Dev Mode's "Ready for development" status to approved frames.

## Access

All operations go through the **Figma MCP server**, driven via `figma-use`. No direct REST calls.

**Preflight (first operation of a session):** read [figma-use/SKILL.md](../figma-use/SKILL.md), verify the MCP server is reachable and authenticated, and verify file **{{FIGMA_FILE}}** is accessible.

## Structure

Two kinds of content in one file. That's the whole model.

- **A `Components` page** — everything used in more than one place, plus the variables they're built from. This *is* the design system. No separate library file, no page per category, no atomic hierarchy.
- **Design pages** — add one when a feature needs somewhere to live, named after that feature. Frames are the screens and states, named for what they are. No required pages, no sections, no numbering scheme.

Components are used **locally, in the same file** — no import/publish round-trip for internal work. Reach for **publish design library** only when another Figma file needs to consume them. Re-publish after each change, with a note.

### Read the page list through the plugin API

Warning: `get_metadata` called with a `fileKey` and no `nodeId` returns the **first page only**. Its own description says it returns a list of the top-level pages in the document. It does not.

A one-page answer looks like a complete answer. Nothing signals that the other pages are missing. Measured 2026-08-31 on a file holding 11 pages: the tool reported one, and an audit that trusted it stated the file held no screens. The file held 35 frames.

List the pages through `use_figma`:

```js
return figma.root.children.map(p => ({ id: p.id, name: p.name }));
```

Pages load incrementally, so a page's children are not readable until the page is current. To read one page, get it with `getNodeByIdAsync`, then `await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page)`, then read `page.children`. Set the current page once per `use_figma` call. For several pages, fan the calls out in parallel.

## Components

- Built from variables — no hardcoded colors, sizes, or typography.
- Use **auto layout** so they respond to content.
- Add **variants** for the axes that genuinely vary (size, style, state). Interactive components define the states they actually have.
- Build at the **real rendered sizes of the implemented product**, not assumed values — and confirm tints and borders are actually visible in a screenshot, not just present in the layer list.

## Tokens

- Figma **Variables**, named semantically (`color.surface.primary`, `spacing.large`). One flat layer unless there's a concrete reason for more.
- Light/dark are **Variable modes**, not duplicate token sets.
- **Store tints and overlays as pre-blended solid colors**, not a base color at reduced opacity — paint-level opacity on a variable binding does not survive a library publish, and instances render fully opaque.
- Values that **vary by mode** only resolve correctly where the consuming page or frame sets its mode explicitly. Set the mode before placing instances.

## Code map

A checked-in `figma-code-map.json` in the consuming repo links each Figma component to the code component that implements it — a free stand-in for Figma Code Connect (which needs a paid Dev seat). It is a **reference, not a sync**: it never edits code or Figma, it just lets design and dev point at each other. Worth keeping current.

**Format** — defined by the JSON Schema at `.agents/tools/figma-code-map.schema.json`. Make `"$schema": "./.agents/tools/figma-code-map.schema.json"` the first key for editor autocomplete. Each entry carries the code component's name and path plus the Figma component name(s) and node id(s) (`<num>:<num>`) it corresponds to. One code component may map from several Figma components — different variant sets or representations of the same thing.

**When to update it** — whenever you **add, rename, move, or remove** a component, on either side. Adding a component in Figma without adding its entry is the common way the map goes stale.

**How to use it** — before building something new, check the map: if a Figma component already maps to code, reuse or extend that code component instead of reinventing it. Cite the mapped code component in handoff so developers know exactly what to touch.

**Validate after every edit** — run the linter and fix every failure:

```bash
.agents/tools/figma-code-map-lint.sh   # defaults to ./figma-code-map.json
```

It validates against the schema and against the project — paths exist, code names are unique, node ids well-formed — and **fails** (non-zero exit) on any of those. It also **warns** about sibling source files with no entry. Treat each warning as a prompt to either map the component or add a documented entry saying why it isn't mapped. Pure `bash` + `jq`. If the project has no `figma-code-map.json`, there's nothing to lint.

## Handoff

- **Mark frame ready for development** in place — the Dev Mode status *is* the signal. No separate handoff page.
- Keep layer names, component properties, and variables meaningful. Dev Mode is the developer's interface to the design.
- Annotate interaction intent, state transitions, and accessibility requirements that aren't self-evident from the visual.
- **Verify each screen with the user**, not just your own render — subtle defects (invisible borders, dropped opacity, wrong variable mode) hide from a screenshot.
